   "date": "2020-08-18T16:35:07Z",
   "subject": "Clarification on combing space and time components",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Most of the literature I have read combines space and time components in a single f() call using the group parameter:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Intercept</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> f</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> spde</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                                    control</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;ar1&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>             data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">full_stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>             control</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">predictor </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">full_stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>                                      compute </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><br>However, I have also seen somewhere of doing this in two f() calls:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Intercept</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> f</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> spde</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> f</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;ar1&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>             data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">full_stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>             control</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">predictor </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">full_stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>                                      compute </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div>Can someone explain to me how these are different? It seems like the first method creates an interaction of space and time through the Kronecker Product but I cannot tell if this means that each space component has as its own time AR1 coefficient to be estimated.<br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "VNYxBYzvBQAJ",
   "from": "Finn Lindgren",
   "author": "afb003e064114795",
   "date": "2020-08-18T17:28:34Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Clarification on combing space and time components",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>the short answer:</div><div><br></div><div>Let Cs and Ct be the covariances of a purely spatial model and a purely temporal model.</div><div><br></div><div>A model that using two f() components gives a functional additive model,<br></div><div>  \\eta(s,t) = f1(s) + f2(t)</div><div>and the combined space-time covariance matrix can be written</div><div>  C = Ct \\otimes 1 + 1 \\otimes Cs</div><div>i.e. the sum of kronecker products, where 1 are all-one matrices of required size;</div><div>the spatial function is constant over time, and the temporal function is constant across space.</div><div><br></div><div>A model using a single f() with group gives a joint model</div><div>  \\eta(s,t) = f(s,t)</div><div>with space-time covariance matrix</div><div>  C = Ct \\otimes Cs</div><div><br></div><div>The first version is separable on a functional level.</div><div>The second version is only separable on the covariance level.</div><div>Both use the same set of parameters (except that the second version only needs one common precision scaling parameter).</div><div>  </div><div>Finn</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Tue, 18 Aug 2020 at 17:35, Ed Levinson &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">levin...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
